INT. DIAMOND MERCHANT'S BOARDROOM - NIGHT

Walnut paneling, a table the length of a hearse, a single green-shaded lamp. Rain on tall windows. Under the lamp, a velvet cloth. On it, a diamond the size of a plum.

A JEWELER (70s, loupe on a ribbon, trembling hands, an immaculate black suit) stands behind the table, polishing the stone with a chamois. Across from him sit FLORENCE (40s, severe bob, gray gloves, black coat buttoned to the throat) and NICO (30s, hoodie under a blazer, a nervous habit of rolling a ring on his thumb).

The Jeweler lifts the stone. Light cuts through it, throws a rainbow onto the wall, and for a moment the room is a cathedral.

JEWELER
Three hundred and eleven carats. Rough, it weighed six hundred.

FLORENCE
A good cut.

JEWELER
A good cut is the difference between a stone and a legend.

NICO
It's beautiful, Mr. Silverman.

JEWELER
Abraham. Please. Nobody has called me Mr. Silverman since my wife died.

FLORENCE
Abraham, then. Shall we discuss the price?

ABRAHAM
Of course.

He sets the diamond back on its cloth. He sits. His hands, which had trembled, are now perfectly still.

ABRAHAM (CONT'D)
My understanding is that you represent a buyer in Antwerp.

FLORENCE
We represent several.

ABRAHAM
But only one with the money.

FLORENCE
The buyer will pay fifty million, in escrow, on delivery.

ABRAHAM
Delivery where?

FLORENCE
Where you specify.

ABRAHAM
Here.

NICO
Here?

ABRAHAM
Here. This room. Tonight.

Nico glances at Florence. Florence doesn't move.

FLORENCE
That wasn't the arrangement.

ABRAHAM
The arrangement has changed. I find, in my old age, that I enjoy having my business conducted in a room I trust.

FLORENCE
Mr. Silverman...

ABRAHAM
Abraham.

FLORENCE
Abraham. We are trying to be reasonable.

ABRAHAM
So am I. I am sixty-nine years old. I have outlived two wives and three partners. I know what a man looks like when he is about to rob me.

Silence. Nico stops rolling the ring. Florence tilts her head, an inch, no more.

FLORENCE
And do I look like a man?

ABRAHAM
You look like someone who has read the floor plan.

NICO
Florence.

FLORENCE
Quiet.

ABRAHAM
Please. I would hate to be rude. But you will notice that the lamp is the only light in the room.

Florence looks up. The windows have gone dark. The curtains are drawn. At the door, a soft click: the deadbolt.

ABRAHAM (CONT'D)
Nico, may I call you Nico?

NICO
(thin)
Yes.

ABRAHAM
You have a very nice face. I would hate for it to be on the news.

Nico stands. His chair scrapes. Florence puts a hand on his arm, firm.

FLORENCE
Sit.

NICO
He knows.

FLORENCE
Of course he knows. He's known since Tuesday.

She turns to Abraham. For the first time, she smiles.

FLORENCE (CONT'D)
So tell me, Abraham. What do you want?

ABRAHAM
Thirty percent.

FLORENCE
Of what?

ABRAHAM
Of whatever you were going to take.

A long beat. Florence peels off one glove, finger by finger. Lays it on the velvet beside the diamond.

FLORENCE
Twenty.

ABRAHAM
Twenty-five, and I stay out of the photograph.

FLORENCE
Done.

She extends her bare hand. Abraham takes it. The lamp flickers, and the stone throws its rainbow across all three of them.
